// A `#[Transient]` field of a type that derives `Deserialize` must be fillable **without the wire**,
// because a decode of that type's own output has nothing to read for it: a `?T` (`none`) or a
// default the checker could fold to a literal. Anything else is refused at the declaration.
//
// Asked here rather than at the first parse, which is the point of the case. A type marked this way
// encodes perfectly and fails only when something reads its output back — at whichever runtime does
// it first, naming a field the author deliberately removed from the wire. That reads as a decoder
// bug; the missing default it actually is has to be said where the default would go.
//
// Three shapes fail, one per reason a fill can be impossible: no default at all, a default the
// checker could not fold (`= size()` runs an expression, and a decode is a data walk), and a literal
// default of a type that has no JSON form to decode it through.
// expect: error E0050 at 19:26
// expect: error E0050 at 25:26
// expect: error E0050 at 31:26
// expect: exit 1
use std.json.Transient

@derive(Serialize<Json>, Deserialize<Json>)
struct Order {
    id: string
    #[Transient] note: string
}

@derive(Serialize<Json>, Deserialize<Json>)
struct Ticket {
    id: string
    #[Transient] at: int = size()
}

@derive(Serialize<Json>, Deserialize<Json>)
struct Batch {
    id: string
    #[Transient] tags: Set<string> = #{}
}

fn size(): int { return 1; }
